-- How to Use Zoom to Run Webinars
update public.lessons set
  learning_objectives = array['Tell a meeting apart from a webinar, and know that the webinar format is a paid add-on rather than a setting you switch on', 'Set the security defaults and the host roles that stop an event being hijacked or derailed', 'Plan for the three things that actually end a Kenyan webinar: connection, power and data - including a backup host'],
  notes = 'Before any settings, the distinction that decides what you can promise a client.

A MEETING AND A WEBINAR ARE DIFFERENT PRODUCTS

In a MEETING everybody can unmute, turn on a camera and be seen by everyone. That is right for a workshop of twelve people and wrong for a presentation to two hundred.

In a WEBINAR the audience cannot unmute or appear on camera. Only the host and the panellists are seen and heard. The audience interacts through questions, chat and polls. You get registration, reporting on who attended and for how long, and a practice session before going live.

The part people discover late: THE WEBINAR FORMAT IS A SEPARATE PAID ADD-ON on top of a paid plan, priced by how many attendees you need to admit. It is not a toggle on a free account.

So check what the client actually needs before quoting. A free or basic account running a MEETING with the audience muted and video disabled on entry handles a surprising number of events perfectly well, and costs nothing. Reach for the webinar add-on when you genuinely need registration, large numbers and a locked-down audience - and put its cost in the quote.

THE FREE TIER''S LIMITS, WHICH ARE REAL

Free accounts cap meeting length for group calls - historically around forty minutes - and that clock has ended many events mid-sentence. Two honest options: pay for the month you need, or design around it by scheduling a short session and telling people in advance there is a deliberate break.

Never plan a one-hour event on a forty-minute account hoping it will be fine.

THE ROLES, AND WHY THEY MATTER

- HOST. Full control. Only one at a time.
- CO-HOST. Can mute people, admit from the waiting room, manage chat. Assign at least one, ALWAYS, which is the single most valuable preparation in this lesson - see the outage section.
- PANELLIST. Can speak and share video. Your speakers.
- ATTENDEE. Watches, asks questions, answers polls.
- ALTERNATIVE HOST. Can start the event without you.

Decide and assign these BEFORE the event, not while two hundred people wait.

SECURITY: THE DEFAULTS THAT PREVENT A DISASTER

Events get hijacked by people sharing offensive material or shouting over a speaker. Every one of those is preventable with settings you choose in advance:

- SCREEN SHARING: HOST ONLY. This is the one that matters most. An attendee who can share their screen can show the room anything.
- WAITING ROOM ON, or registration required, so nobody arrives unvetted.
- MUTE PARTICIPANTS ON ENTRY, and do not allow them to unmute themselves in a large event.
- DO NOT POST THE LINK PUBLICLY. Use registration so each person gets their own link, or at minimum require a passcode.
- LOCK THE EVENT once everybody expected has arrived.
- KNOW WHERE THE REMOVE BUTTON IS before you need it, and make sure a co-host does too.

Set these when you schedule, not when something goes wrong.

RECORDING, AND CONSENT

You can record locally to your own machine or to the provider''s cloud if your plan includes it. Local recording costs you nothing and uses your disk; cloud recording has a storage limit.

Two things to get right:

- TELL PEOPLE IT IS BEING RECORDED, out loud at the start and in the registration page. The platform usually announces it too; say it anyway.
- A recording of a session is personal data about the people in it. Decide before the event how long you will keep it, who can see it, and whether you will publish it. If attendees are identifiable, say so when you ask them to register. Kenya''s Data Protection Act 2019 applies to the registration list and the recording alike; confirm current obligations with the Office of the Data Protection Commissioner rather than assuming a small event is exempt.

QUESTIONS AND POLLS: USE BOTH, FOR DIFFERENT THINGS

- Q AND A collects questions properly, lets a co-host triage them, and lets you answer the good ones rather than the loudest.
- CHAT is for conversation and links, and needs a co-host watching it.
- POLLS are the cheapest way to wake a room up. Write them BEFORE the event; writing one live while people watch is painful.

THE THREE THINGS THAT ACTUALLY END A KENYAN WEBINAR

This section is the reason this lesson exists, and no international tutorial covers it.

1. CONNECTION. Video calling needs a connection that holds for the whole hour, not one that averages well. As presenter you need a stable upload, and the next lesson explains why upload is the number that matters. Test at the hour of your event, on the connection you will actually use.

2. POWER. An outage mid-event is not a rare accident; plan for it. A charged laptop plus a charged phone as a hotspot is the minimum. And this is why a CO-HOST OR ALTERNATIVE HOST IS ESSENTIAL: if your power dies, the event continues with them instead of ending. Brief them beforehand on exactly what to say.

3. DATA, FOR YOUR AUDIENCE. A video call costs the attendee roughly 300 to 600 MB an hour at ordinary quality, more at high definition. Many of your attendees are on bundles. So:

- Tell people they can join AUDIO ONLY, and tell them how. It cuts their data use dramatically and most do not know it is possible.
- Share the dial-in option if your plan has it, for people whose data is finished.
- Keep slides simple rather than video-heavy.
- Send the slides and a recording afterwards, so somebody who could not stay is not excluded.

That last one converts goodwill into attendance next time.

THE PRACTICE SESSION, WHICH IS FREE

The webinar format lets the host and panellists enter early in a practice session the audience cannot see. Use it every single time: check each speaker''s audio, their screen share, their camera, and that they know how to unmute. Five minutes there prevents the first five minutes of your event being a sound check in front of your audience.',
  practice_activity = 'Set one event up properly and rehearse the failure you will actually face.

Step 1. Decide the format on evidence. Write the answers:

- Expected attendees: ____
- Do they need to speak or be seen? ____
- Do you need registration and attendance reporting? ____
- Therefore: meeting or webinar add-on? ____
- Cost of that choice per month in shillings: ____

Step 2. Check the length limit on the account you will actually use, and write it. Then write your event''s planned duration and confirm it fits.

Step 3. Assign the roles by name before anything else: host, at least one co-host, each panellist, and an alternative host. Write the names.

Step 4. Set every security option and write down what you chose for each: screen sharing, waiting room, mute on entry, whether attendees may unmute, how the link is distributed, and when you will lock the event. Then find the remove-participant button and confirm your co-host can too.

Step 5. Write your recording decision in three lines: local or cloud, how long you keep it, who can see it. Then write the sentence you will say aloud at the start, and one line naming what you will confirm with the Office of the Data Protection Commissioner about the registration list and the recording.

Step 6. Write two polls in advance. Actual questions, actual options.

Step 7. Test the connection at the real hour. Hold a thirty-minute call with a co-host at the time your event will run, sharing your screen for part of it. Record whether it held and how much data it used.

Step 8. Rehearse the outage. Write and save:

- Your backup device and backup connection
- The exact message you will send if your power goes
- What your co-host will say and do to keep the event running

Then actually tell your co-host. An unbriefed co-host is not a backup.

Step 9. Write the audio-only instruction you will send attendees in advance, in the words they will understand, and the data figure that explains why.

Step 10. Run the practice session. Check every speaker''s audio, screen share and camera. Write down what you found - there is always something.',
  knowledge_check = '[{"question": "A client wants 200 people to watch a presentation without being seen or able to unmute, with registration and attendance reporting. What do they need?", "options": ["A free account with participants muted on entry", "The webinar format, which is a separate paid add-on priced by attendee capacity, on top of a paid plan", "Any paid plan, since webinar mode is just a setting", "Two meetings running at once"], "correct_index": 1}, {"question": "Why is assigning a co-host or alternative host the single most valuable preparation?", "options": ["It allows more people to share their screens", "It increases the attendee limit", "Because if your power or connection fails, the event continues with them instead of ending", "It removes the free-tier time limit"], "correct_index": 2}]'::jsonb
where id = 'c667e25c-eae3-4032-8f81-c2a29d283c8e';

-- Start Live Streaming With StreamYard
update public.lessons set
  learning_objectives = array['Explain why a browser-based studio suits guests on weak devices, and what the free tier costs you in branding and hours', 'Measure your UPLOAD speed and set a bitrate and resolution that hold, since upload is the constraint on Kenyan connections', 'Stream to the platform your audience already uses rather than asking them to install anything, and keep a local backup'],
  notes = 'A browser-based live studio does something the previous lesson''s tool does not: it puts your branding, overlays and name bars on a stream and sends it to Facebook, YouTube and LinkedIn at the same time, with nothing to install.

WHY NO INSTALL MATTERS MORE THAN IT SOUNDS

Your guest joins by clicking a link in a browser. No account, no download, no setup call the day before.

That single fact removes the commonest cause of a late start: a guest who could not install the software, could not find their password, or is on a borrowed machine where they cannot install anything. For interviews and panels with people who are not technical, this is worth more than any feature.

The trade is that a browser doing live video is hungry. Close every other tab, close other applications, and use a laptop rather than a phone if you have the choice.

THE FREE TIER, HONESTLY

Terms change, so check the current pricing page. The shape has been: a free tier with a limited number of streaming hours a month and the provider''s WATERMARK on your stream, and paid tiers that remove the branding and raise the limits.

The watermark is the thing to notice. For your own content it is harmless. For a CLIENT''S event it looks unprofessional and they will see it, so a client stream needs the paid tier - and that cost belongs in the quote, in shillings, before you agree a price.

UPLOAD IS THE NUMBER THAT MATTERS, AND NOBODY CHECKS IT

Here is the single most useful thing in this lesson. When you watch video you DOWNLOAD. When you stream video you UPLOAD. Most Kenyan connections - mobile data and many home packages - are asymmetric: download is several times faster than upload.

So the 20 Mbps your package advertises may be 3 Mbps up, and the speed test you ran reassured you about entirely the wrong number.

What to do:

- RUN A SPEED TEST AND READ THE UPLOAD FIGURE. Write it down. Then run it again at the hour your event will actually happen, because evening contention is real.
- MATCH YOUR RESOLUTION TO IT. As a working guide, 1080p streaming wants something like 5 Mbps of stable upload; 720p is comfortable at around 2.5 to 3. Those are approximations, and STABLE matters more than the peak - a connection that averages 5 but drops to 1 every minute will stutter where a steady 3 will not.
- CHOOSE 720p WITHOUT EMBARRASSMENT. A steady 720p stream looks professional. A 1080p stream that buffers looks broken, and your audience is mostly watching on a phone where the difference is invisible anyway.
- WIRE IN IF YOU CAN. Ethernet over wifi, every time. If wifi is the only option, sit beside the router, not two rooms away.
- STOP EVERYTHING ELSE USING THE CONNECTION. Somebody else in the house streaming video will take your headroom. Say so in advance.

WHAT THE STUDIO ACTUALLY GIVES YOU

- BRANDING: your logo, your colours, a background, and lower thirds with names and titles. This is what makes a stream look like a production rather than a video call.
- LAYOUTS: one speaker, side by side, a grid, a speaker with a shared screen. Switch between them live.
- COMMENTS ON SCREEN: pull a viewer''s comment from the platform onto the stream. This is the single best way to make a live audience feel present, and it costs nothing.
- A BACKSTAGE AREA where guests wait before you bring them on.

Set the branding up BEFORE the event. Doing it live while people watch is the same mistake as writing a poll live.

MULTISTREAMING: GO WHERE THE AUDIENCE ALREADY IS

You can send one stream to several platforms at once. The important consequence is strategic, not technical.

A webinar asks people to register, receive a link, and show up in an application. A live stream on Facebook or YouTube appears in a place they are already sitting, needs no installation, costs them less data to join, and notifies their followers when it starts.

For a public event aimed at a wide audience, that is a far lower barrier. Keep the registration-based webinar for events where you need the attendee list and a controlled room; use the stream where reach matters more than the list.

Note two things about multistreaming: it uses MORE upload bandwidth per destination on some setups, so if your upload is marginal, stream to one platform rather than three. And check each platform''s rules - some restrict simultaneous streaming, and some restrict what you may charge for.

RECORD LOCALLY, ALWAYS

Whatever else you do, record a copy. Streams fail, platforms have outages, and a recording is the thing you send to everybody who missed it - which the follow-up lesson shows is where much of the value sits.

If the studio records to the cloud, download it the same day rather than leaving it on a plan whose storage expires.

THE RUN-UP, IN ORDER

1. Set the branding, overlays and lower thirds days before.
2. Run a PRIVATE test stream to an unlisted destination, with your actual guest, on your actual connection, at the actual hour. This is not optional; it is the whole insurance policy.
3. Check audio first, as always. Bad audio loses a live audience faster than anything.
4. Brief guests: close other tabs, use earphones to stop echo, sit facing a window, and stay on the link.
5. Start the stream a few minutes early with a holding frame so early arrivals see something deliberate.

AUDIO, ONCE MORE

Everybody on the stream should wear earphones. Without them, the speaker''s voice comes out of their laptop, back into their microphone, and the whole stream echoes. It is the commonest fault on amateur streams and it is solved by a KSh 200 pair of earphones.',
  practice_activity = 'Measure what actually constrains you, then run a private test before anything public.

Step 1. Run a speed test and write BOTH numbers:

- Download: ____ Mbps
- UPLOAD: ____ Mbps

Then run it again at the hour your event will run and write the upload figure a second time. Note the difference.

Step 2. Choose your resolution from that upload figure, not from ambition. Write your choice and the reason.

Step 3. Price the tier honestly:

- Free tier hours per month and whether it watermarks: ____
- Paid tier cost per month in shillings: ____
- Which you need for a CLIENT event, and why: ____

Step 4. Set up the branding before any test: logo, colours, background, and a lower third with a real name and title. Write what you built.

Step 5. Brief one real guest and have them join by link in a browser. Write down how long it took them from link to on-screen, and whether anything blocked them.

Step 6. Fix the echo. Confirm every person on the stream is wearing earphones. Then deliberately have one person remove theirs and listen to what happens, so you recognise it instantly in future.

Step 7. Run a PRIVATE test stream to an unlisted destination, for ten minutes, with your guest, at the real hour, on the real connection. Record:

- Did it hold without stuttering? ____
- Was the audio clean? ____
- What broke, and what you changed: ____

Step 8. Pull one comment from the platform onto the stream and write one sentence on why this matters for a live audience.

Step 9. Decide where you will stream and write the reason in terms of your audience, not the technology. Then write whether you will multistream or send to one destination, referring back to your upload figure.

Step 10. Record locally, download the recording the same day, and write where you stored it and where the second copy lives.',
  knowledge_check = '[{"question": "Your package advertises 20 Mbps and your 1080p stream keeps stuttering. What have you most likely overlooked?", "options": ["The platform is throttling you", "Streaming uses UPLOAD, which on most Kenyan connections is several times slower than download - so the relevant figure was never checked", "The camera resolution is too low", "Browser-based studios cannot stream at 1080p"], "correct_index": 1}, {"question": "Your whole stream echoes whenever a guest speaks. What is the cause and fix?", "options": ["The bitrate is too high; lower the resolution", "The platform needs a different layout", "A guest''s speaker output is feeding back into their microphone - everyone on the stream must wear earphones", "The stream must be recorded locally instead of to the cloud"], "correct_index": 2}]'::jsonb
where id = 'd9c37e7d-84df-4a8b-9cec-8f500b0bfb2e';

-- How to Get More Webinar Registrants & Attendees
update public.lessons set
  learning_objectives = array['Plan around the honest registration-to-attendance rate rather than assuming everyone who signs up will come', 'Write a registration page and a reminder sequence that actually work, using WhatsApp where it beats email', 'Choose partners, timing and a token price deliberately, and collect contact details lawfully'],
  notes = 'Two different problems get treated as one: getting people to REGISTER, and getting registrants to TURN UP. The second is where events are lost.

THE NUMBER TO PLAN AROUND

Of the people who register for a free webinar, expect roughly a THIRD TO A HALF to attend live. That is normal and it is not a reflection on you.

So if a client needs fifty people in the room, you need somewhere between a hundred and a hundred and fifty registrations. Say that at the planning stage, in writing, because a client who was promised "fifty registrations" and sees twenty attendees will think the event failed.

Attendance also falls over the length of the event. People leave. Put the most valuable thing in the first half, not the last ten minutes.

WHAT MAKES SOMEBODY REGISTER

Four things, in order of importance:

1. A SPECIFIC OUTCOME IN THE TITLE. Not "Digital Marketing Webinar" but "How to get your first five customers from WhatsApp, without paying for ads". The specific version names a result the person wants. The general version names a category.
2. A NAMED HOST WITH A REASON TO BE LISTENED TO. One sentence: what they have done, with a number in it.
3. A SPECIFIC TIME, stated with the date and the day. "Thursday 14th, 7pm" is a commitment; "next week" is not.
4. A SHORT FORM. Name and one contact detail. Every extra field loses people, and asking for a job title, a company and a phone number on a free event loses a lot of them.

Three more things that help: a clear list of three things they will be able to do afterwards, the duration stated honestly, and a promise that the recording will be sent - which both increases registration and reduces the pressure to attend, so use it knowing the trade.

REMINDERS ARE THE SINGLE BIGGEST LEVER

Most no-shows are not disinterest. They are people who meant to come and forgot. Reminders fix a large share of that, and they are nearly free.

Send three:

- 24 HOURS BEFORE, with the link and what they will learn.
- 1 HOUR BEFORE, short, with the link.
- AT THE MOMENT YOU GO LIVE, one line: we are starting now, here is the link.

That last one feels excessive and it is the highest-performing message of the three.

AND IN KENYA, WHATSAPP OUTPERFORMS EMAIL for this, substantially. People read WhatsApp within minutes and email within days, if at all.

But do it properly:

- Use a BROADCAST LIST or a message to people who gave you their number for this purpose - not a group you added them to. Adding somebody to a group without asking is the fastest way to be reported and blocked, and it is the same mistake the affiliate and tutoring lessons warn about.
- Keep the message short and put the link at the end.
- Collect the number WITH CONSENT at registration, saying clearly what you will use it for. That consent is both a legal requirement under Kenya''s Data Protection Act 2019 and the thing that makes the message welcome rather than intrusive. Confirm current obligations with the Office of the Data Protection Commissioner rather than assuming.

AND NEVER BUY A LIST. Messaging people who never gave you their details is unlawful, gets your number blocked, and does not work anyway.

TIMING, FOR A KENYAN AUDIENCE

- EVENINGS, from about 7pm, catch working people after they are home and fed.
- LUNCHTIME works for office audiences and is short - keep it to forty minutes.
- SATURDAY MORNINGS work for students and people running their own businesses.
- Avoid Friday evenings, and avoid anything scheduled against a major match or a national event.

And remember the time zone if you have an international audience, stating the time with its zone so nobody arrives an hour out.

WHERE REGISTRATIONS ACTUALLY COME FROM

In order of what works:

- A PARTNER OR CO-HOST WITH THEIR OWN AUDIENCE. The highest-leverage thing on this list. One co-host who shares it with their community can double your registrations, and they get the exposure in return. Ask specifically, naming what you want them to send and when.
- YOUR OWN LIST OR COMMUNITY. People who already know you.
- A WHATSAPP COMMUNITY WHERE THE TOPIC IS GENUINELY ON TOPIC, posted once, where the rules allow it.
- SOCIAL POSTS, repeated - once is not promotion. Post the specific outcome, not "join my webinar".
- PAID PROMOTION, last, and only once an event has proved it converts organically.

THE TOKEN FEE, WHICH RAISES ATTENDANCE

A free event costs nothing to skip. A small fee - even a few hundred shillings by M-Pesa - changes the arithmetic of a person''s evening and raises attendance substantially.

Two honest caveats: it cuts registrations, sometimes sharply, and it adds payment admin. So use it where attendance matters more than reach, and keep the free route for events whose purpose is to be seen by as many people as possible.

A middle option that works well: free to attend, with a paid option for something extra - the templates, the recording with the Q and A, a follow-up session.

THE PAGE, AND THE THING MOST PEOPLE GET WRONG

Whatever platform hosts your registration, the page must answer, in the first screenful on a PHONE: what will I be able to do, who is teaching, when exactly, how long, and what do I type to register.

Test it on a cheap phone before you send it anywhere. A registration page that requires scrolling past a large image to find the date loses people who were ready to sign up.',
  practice_activity = 'Plan one real event''s promotion with the honest numbers.

Step 1. Work backwards from the room:

- Attendees the event needs: ____
- Registrations required at a 35 percent show-up rate: ____
- Registrations required at 50 percent: ____

Write the range you will quote, and the sentence you will say to a client so they are not surprised later.

Step 2. Write the title twice: once as a category, once as a specific outcome. Keep the second. Read both aloud and note the difference.

Step 3. Write the host line in one sentence, with a number in it.

Step 4. Set the exact time with day, date and zone, chosen from the timing section with a stated reason for your audience.

Step 5. Build the registration page and list every field you ask for. Then delete every field you do not genuinely need, and write how many you removed.

Step 6. Test the page on the cheapest phone you can borrow. Write down whether the outcome, host, time and sign-up action are all visible without scrolling past an image. Fix it if not.

Step 7. Write all three reminders, word for word: 24 hours, 1 hour, and going-live. Keep the last one to one line.

Step 8. Decide your channel honestly. Write which of email and WhatsApp your audience actually reads, and how you will collect numbers WITH consent. Write the consent wording you will put on the registration form, and one line naming what you will confirm with the Office of the Data Protection Commissioner.

Step 9. Name one real partner or co-host with their own audience. Write the message you will send asking them, naming exactly what you want them to share and when.

Step 10. Decide on the fee. Write free, token fee, or free with a paid extra - and the reason, referring to whether this event needs reach or attendance.',
  knowledge_check = '[{"question": "A client needs 60 people in the room for a free webinar. Roughly how many registrations should you plan for?", "options": ["About 60, since registration shows intent", "About 120 to 180, because only a third to a half of registrants typically attend live", "About 70, allowing a small margin", "It cannot be estimated"], "correct_index": 1}, {"question": "Which single action recovers the most no-shows?", "options": ["A longer, more detailed registration page", "Paid social promotion in the final week", "A short reminder sent at the moment you go live, on the channel your audience actually reads", "Extending the event by thirty minutes"], "correct_index": 2}]'::jsonb
where id = 'ad0a3335-cc49-474b-9b85-98be4d2a4cc2';

-- How to Plan and Host a Successful Virtual Event
update public.lessons set
  learning_objectives = array['Write a minute-by-minute run sheet with named roles, so nobody is deciding anything live', 'Prepare a written failure plan for the five things that actually go wrong, and rehearse on the real connection', 'Keep the event short, interactive and accessible, and handle the attendee list as the personal data it is'],
  notes = 'A good virtual event is almost entirely preparation. What the audience experiences as smooth is somebody having decided everything in advance.

THE RUN SHEET

Write the event minute by minute, on one page, and give it to everybody involved. A workable shape for a sixty-minute session:

- MINUS 30: host and panellists in the practice session. Audio, screen share, camera checked for each.
- MINUS 10: doors open. Holding screen up with the title, the start time and one line of instruction.
- MINUS 5: a co-host greets arrivals in chat by name.
- 0 to 3: welcome, who you are, what they will be able to do by the end, housekeeping - recorded, questions in Q and A, audio-only option.
- 3 to 8: a poll. Immediately. It makes the room active rather than passive and tells you who is there.
- 8 to 40: the content, in three clear parts, with a question or a poll between each.
- 40 to 52: questions, triaged by the co-host so you answer the useful ones.
- 52 to 57: the one thing you want them to do next, stated once, clearly.
- 57 to 60: thank them, say what they will receive and when, end.

Note what is in that sheet: interaction every eight to ten minutes, the valuable content early, and the call to action spoken once rather than repeatedly.

ROLES, NAMED, NOT ASSUMED

- HOST, who speaks.
- MODERATOR, who watches chat, greets people, triages questions, and reads the good ones out.
- TECHNICAL, who admits people, manages the recording, and handles the thing that breaks.

One person can do two of these for a small event. Nobody can do all three for a large one, and trying is how an event ends with a presenter talking over their own screen share while fifty unanswered questions pile up.

KEEP IT SHORT, AND ABOUT ONE THING

Forty-five to sixty minutes, one topic, three parts. Attendance decays, attention decays faster, and an event that runs ninety minutes is an event most people leave before the end.

If you have two topics, you have two events - and a reason for people to come back.

THE FAILURE PLAN, WRITTEN BEFORE THE EVENT

Five things go wrong. Decide the response now, in writing, and give it to your co-host:

1. A PRESENTER DROPS OFF. The moderator fills, says plainly that we are reconnecting, and moves to the Q and A early rather than letting silence run.
2. YOUR POWER OR CONNECTION DIES. Your co-host continues - the previous lessons insisted on this. You rejoin from your phone. The event does not end.
3. SCREEN SHARE FAILS. Have the slides open as a PDF on a second device, and be ready to present without them. Knowing your material well enough to continue slideless is the real backup.
4. AUDIO ECHOES. The moderator identifies who and asks them privately in chat to use earphones or mute. One line, not a discussion.
5. ALMOST NOBODY COMES. Run it properly anyway, for the people who did. They came; they deserve the full event, and the recording serves everyone else. A host who visibly deflates in front of eight people loses those eight too.

THE DRY RUN, ON THE REAL CONNECTION

Twenty-four hours before, run the whole thing: the same platform, the same connection, the same hour, every speaker present. Not a chat about it - an actual rehearsal of the first ten minutes and any transition.

What this catches: a speaker who cannot share their screen, slides that are unreadable at the size the audience will see, a transition nobody had agreed, a connection that is fine at 2pm and unusable at 7pm.

ACCESSIBILITY, WHICH IS ALSO JUST GOOD PRACTICE

- SPEAK CLEARLY AND NOT TOO FAST, especially with an audience whose first language may differ from yours.
- SAY OUT LOUD WHAT IS ON THE SCREEN. Somebody is listening on a phone in a matatu, and somebody else cannot see it well.
- HIGH CONTRAST, LARGE TYPE, FEW WORDS PER SLIDE. Design for a phone screen, as the motion-graphics lesson insists for titles.
- TURN ON LIVE CAPTIONS if the platform offers them, and say they are available.
- SEND THE SLIDES AFTERWARDS so nobody had to copy frantically.

THE ATTENDEE LIST IS PERSONAL DATA

You will finish the event holding names, email addresses, phone numbers, the questions people asked and how long each person stayed. That is personal data about identifiable people, and you have obligations:

- Use it only for what you said you would use it for at registration.
- Do NOT share the attendee list with a sponsor or partner unless you said at registration that you would and people agreed.
- Keep it somewhere access-controlled, not in a shared folder or a group chat.
- Delete it when you no longer need it, and decide "when" in advance.

Kenya''s Data Protection Act 2019 governs this. Confirm the current requirements with the Office of the Data Protection Commissioner rather than assuming an event list is too small to matter.

THE TEN MINUTES BEFORE

Doors open early with a holding screen, so the first arrivals see something deliberate rather than an empty room or your desktop. Say on it: the title, that you start at a stated minute, and that they should check their audio. Quiet background music if your platform and your rights allow it - and note that playing commercial music on a recorded, published stream is a licensing question, not a free choice.

Then start on time. Starting late to wait for latecomers punishes the people who were punctual, and they are the ones who will come back.',
  practice_activity = 'Write the run sheet and the failure plan, then rehearse for real.

Step 1. Write your run sheet minute by minute on one page, using the shape in the notes. Mark where each poll and each question break falls, and confirm there is interaction at least every ten minutes.

Step 2. Assign the three roles by name: host, moderator, technical. If one person covers two, write which two and why that is workable at your expected size.

Step 3. Check the shape of your content. Write your one topic, your three parts, and the total minutes. If it exceeds sixty, write which part becomes a second event.

Step 4. Write the failure plan for all five situations from the notes, with the exact words your moderator will use for the first two. Send it to them.

Step 5. Prepare the screen-share backup: slides as a PDF on a second device, and write one line confirming you could deliver the session without slides at all.

Step 6. Run the dry run 24 hours before, on the same platform, same connection, same hour, with every speaker. Write down three things it caught.

Step 7. Check your slides for accessibility. View them at phone size and write how many words your busiest slide has. Reduce it. Then write the sentence you will use to describe what is on screen for people who cannot see it.

Step 8. Build the holding screen: title, start minute, audio check instruction. Write what it says.

Step 9. Write your data plan in four lines: what you collect, what you said you would use it for, where it is stored with access controlled, and the date you will delete it. Add one line naming what you will confirm with the Office of the Data Protection Commissioner, and one line stating that the attendee list will not go to a sponsor unless registrants were told.

Step 10. After the event, write three lines: what went wrong, what the failure plan handled, and the one change to the run sheet for next time. Keep the sheet; it becomes a template.',
  knowledge_check = '[{"question": "Eight people turn up to an event you expected sixty at. What should you do?", "options": ["Cancel and reschedule, since the audience is too small", "Shorten it heavily and skip the interaction", "Run the full event properly for the people who came, and use the recording to serve everyone else", "Ask attendees to invite others before you begin"], "correct_index": 2}, {"question": "After the event you hold names, phone numbers, emails and attendance times. A sponsor asks for the list. What is correct?", "options": ["Share it, since the sponsor funded the event", "Share only the phone numbers", "Do not share it unless registrants were told at registration that it would be shared and agreed - and use the data only for the stated purpose", "Share it after deleting the email addresses"], "correct_index": 2}]'::jsonb
where id = '21970800-1a9e-49f3-906d-bbbc968d3377';

-- This Is How You Follow Up With Webinar Attendees
update public.lessons set
  learning_objectives = array['Send the follow-up within 24 hours, containing the recording, the material and ONE action', 'Write differently to the three groups - attended, registered but absent, and replay watchers - because they are in different situations', 'Collect useful feedback with two questions, and handle the list lawfully afterwards'],
  notes = 'Most of the value of an event is created after it ends, and most people leave it there. The follow-up is cheap, fast, and it is where registrations turn into anything at all.

WITHIN 24 HOURS, NOT NEXT WEEK

Send it the same evening or the following morning. After two days the event has faded and the message reads as an afterthought.

What goes in it, and nothing else:

- A ONE-LINE THANK YOU.
- THE RECORDING. The single most requested thing, and the reason many people registered.
- THE MATERIAL you promised: slides, a checklist, a template, the links you mentioned.
- ONE ACTION. One. Book a call, buy the thing, join the community, reply with a question. A message with four possible next steps produces none.
- ONE QUESTION asking for feedback.

Short. People who attended a sixty-minute event do not want a thousand-word email about it.

THREE GROUPS, THREE MESSAGES

This is the part that is usually skipped, and it is most of the gain.

GROUP 1: ATTENDED LIVE. They know what was covered. Thank them for coming, send the recording and the material, reference something that actually happened - a question somebody asked, a poll result - and state the one action. Referencing the real event is what makes this read as a person rather than an automation.

GROUP 2: REGISTERED BUT DID NOT COME. The biggest group, usually, and the one most often ignored. They were interested enough to sign up; something got in the way. Say plainly: sorry you could not make it, here is the recording, here is what you missed in three bullet points, here is the one action.

Messages to this group are frequently the highest-performing of the three. People do watch recordings, and they are grateful not to have been forgotten.

GROUP 3: WATCHED THE REPLAY LATER. If you can tell who they are, follow up a few days after they watch. They have just consumed the content and are at the same point group 1 was immediately after the event.

If your tooling cannot separate the groups, at minimum separate 1 and 2. That split alone is worth more than any wording you could improve.

HOW TO ASK FOR FEEDBACK SO YOU GET IT

Two questions, not a survey:

1. What was the most useful thing in the session?
2. What did you still have a question about at the end?

The first tells you what to lead with next time. The second is a list of the content your audience wants - it is the cheapest topic research available, and it names your next event for you.

A long form gets a low response and tells you less.

THE RECORDING, PRACTICALLY

Two things specific to a Kenyan audience:

- HOSTING IT SOMEWHERE THEY CAN ACTUALLY WATCH matters. A link that streams at a quality the viewer''s connection can handle - an unlisted video on a major platform, for example - is better than a large file to download. A 1.5 GB download is not a gift to somebody on a bundle.
- SAY HOW LONG IT WILL BE AVAILABLE. If the link expires, say when. If it is permanent, say that too; it is a reason to keep your message.

And edit it lightly if you can: trim the dead first three minutes and the goodbyes. People will watch a tight forty minutes who would abandon a loose sixty.

WHAT TO DO WITH WHAT YOU LEARNED

Write down, the same week, while it is fresh:

- The registration-to-attendance rate you actually got. Keep the number; it makes the next event''s planning honest.
- Where the registrations came from, so you know which channel to repeat.
- The point at which attendance dropped, which tells you what was too long.
- The questions people asked, which are your next three events.

Four numbers and a list. Keep them in the same place every time and after three events you will have something no amount of advice can give you: evidence about your own audience.

THE OBLIGATIONS THAT COME WITH THE LIST

You now hold contact details for people who gave them for ONE purpose - attending an event.

- Use them for what you said. Sending the recording and the material is clearly within that. Adding everyone to a weekly marketing list is not, unless you said so and they agreed.
- MAKE OPTING OUT EASY, in every message, and honour it immediately. One person asking to be removed and still receiving messages is both unlawful and the end of your reputation with their whole network.
- NEVER ADD PEOPLE TO A WHATSAPP GROUP because they attended. Use a broadcast to people who agreed to be contacted. The same rule as the previous lesson, and the same reason.
- Keep the list access-controlled and delete it when its purpose ends.

Kenya''s Data Protection Act 2019 governs the whole of this. Confirm current requirements with the Office of the Data Protection Commissioner rather than assuming.

AND THE ONE THING THAT COMPOUNDS

Ask the people who found it useful to tell one person who would also find it useful. Not a share button - a direct, specific ask in one sentence. A recommendation from an attendee is the highest-converting registration source you will ever have, and it is free.',
  practice_activity = 'Write all three follow-ups before the event, then send them within 24 hours.

Step 1. Decide your ONE action now, before writing anything. One sentence. If you have three candidates, choose and discard the others.

Step 2. Write the message to GROUP 1, who attended. Include the thank you, the recording link, the material, one specific reference to something that happened in the session, the one action, and the feedback question. Keep it under 150 words.

Step 3. Write the message to GROUP 2, who registered and did not come. Include the acknowledgement, the recording, three bullet points of what they missed, and the same one action. Note that this is often the best-performing message.

Step 4. Write the message to GROUP 3, the replay watchers, and note how you will identify them - or write plainly that you cannot, and that you will separate only groups 1 and 2.

Step 5. Sort the list into those groups within 24 hours of the event. Write the size of each group.

Step 6. Prepare the recording properly:

- Trimmed start and end: ____
- Hosted where it streams rather than downloads: ____
- How long it will be available, stated in the message: ____
- File size or quality note for people on bundles: ____

Step 7. Write your two feedback questions exactly as they will appear, and nothing more.

Step 8. Record the four numbers and the list, in a place you will use again:

- Registrations: ____
- Live attendees, and the rate: ____
- Minute at which attendance dropped: ____
- Channel each registration came from: ____
- Every question people asked: ____

Then write the next event''s title from that question list.

Step 9. Write your one-sentence referral ask, and add it to the group 1 message.

Step 10. Write your data lines: what you said the contact details were for, whether a marketing list is within that, how someone opts out, and the date you will delete the list. Add one line naming what you will confirm with the Office of the Data Protection Commissioner.',
  knowledge_check = '[{"question": "Which follow-up group is most often ignored and frequently performs best?", "options": ["People who attended the whole session", "People who registered but did not attend, who signed up out of real interest and are grateful for the recording", "People who watched only the first five minutes", "People who asked a question in the Q and A"], "correct_index": 1}, {"question": "Attendees gave their phone numbers to receive the event link and the recording. What may you do with them?", "options": ["Add everyone to a WhatsApp group for weekly marketing", "Sell the list to a sponsor", "Use them for what was stated - sending the recording and material - with an easy opt-out, and get agreement before any ongoing marketing", "Keep messaging anyone who has not replied until they do"], "correct_index": 2}]'::jsonb
where id = 'cec6cfe9-fdd3-44a6-b4fa-9daa0669eb38';

-- How to Make Money With Webinars
update public.lessons set
  learning_objectives = array['Compare the four ways webinars earn, and say which is the most reliable money in Kenya and why', 'Work the conversion arithmetic honestly, and subtract the costs most people forget', 'Invoice a corporate client properly, and plan for payment terms that arrive long after the work'],
  notes = 'Four business models, and they are not equally good. Naming them properly is most of this lesson.

1. SELL TICKETS TO THE EVENT

People pay to attend. Simplest to understand, smallest ceiling.

- Price in shillings, collected by M-Pesa or a local payment gateway, because a dollar checkout loses most of a Kenyan audience.
- A paid event has far higher attendance per registration, as the promotion lesson explained.
- The ceiling is low: fill a room of fifty at KSh 500 and the event grossed KSh 25,000 before costs, for a week of work.

Good for a specific, valuable, practical session. Not a business on its own.

2. SELL SOMETHING AT THE END

The event is free; it sells a service, a course, a template pack or a consulting engagement.

This is the model most online advice describes, so be honest about the arithmetic:

- 200 registrations
- 40 percent attend: 80 in the room
- 5 percent of attendees buy: 4 sales

Four sales at KSh 5,000 is KSh 20,000. Four at KSh 50,000 is KSh 200,000. The SAME EVENT, so the price of what you sell matters far more than any improvement to the webinar.

Which produces the central insight: this model works when you sell something substantial to a specific audience. It does not work selling a cheap product to a general one, because the conversion rate cannot carry it.

3. SPONSORSHIP

A company pays to be associated with your event and to reach your audience.

Only possible once you have a RELIABLE, SPECIFIC audience - "180 Kenyan small-business owners every month" is sellable; "some people" is not. And note the constraint from the previous lessons: you cannot hand the attendee list to a sponsor unless registrants were told and agreed. Sell exposure, not data.

4. CORPORATE TRAINING DELIVERED BY WEBINAR

This is the most reliable money in Kenya, and it is the one the international advice skips.

Organisations - banks, NGOs, SACCOs, schools, county offices, corporates - have TRAINING BUDGETS. They need staff trained on specific things: spreadsheets, customer service, data protection, digital marketing, report writing. Delivering that remotely is cheaper for them than a venue and travel, and they pay per session or per programme rather than per head.

Why it beats the other three:

- One decision-maker instead of two hundred strangers.
- A real budget already allocated.
- Repeat business: a session that goes well is booked again for the next cohort.
- No marketing funnel at all. You are selling to an organisation, not building an audience.

The skills in this course - a run sheet, roles, a failure plan, a clean stream - are exactly what distinguishes somebody who can be trusted with a company''s staff from somebody who cannot.

THE COSTS MOST PEOPLE FORGET

Subtract all of these before calling anything profit:

- The webinar or streaming platform, monthly, in dollars.
- Your data for the event and the rehearsal.
- The payment gateway''s percentage on ticket sales.
- A speaker''s fee if you brought one.
- Your preparation time, which is usually several times the event length.
- Design or slide work.

A KSh 25,000 ticketed event with a dollar subscription, gateway fees and three days of preparation is not KSh 25,000 of income, and treating it as such is how people conclude webinars do not pay.

INVOICING A CORPORATE CLIENT

This is where the money actually is, so get the mechanics right:

- EXPECT A PROCUREMENT PROCESS. Many organisations issue a local purchase order before work begins. No order, no delivery - that is normal practice and protects you.
- PAYMENT TERMS ARE 30 TO 60 DAYS after invoice, sometimes longer. Plan your cash flow around that. The work being finished does not mean the money has arrived.
- INVOICE PROPERLY: your name or business name, a KRA PIN, the client''s details, a description, the date, the amount, and your bank or M-Pesa details. A rejected invoice restarts the clock.
- VAT: if you are registered for VAT you must charge and account for it; if not, you do not. The registration threshold moves, so confirm your position with KRA rather than assuming.
- WITHHOLDING TAX may be deducted from professional fees before you are paid, and the client issues a certificate for it. Expect to receive less than the invoice total and know why.
- PUT IT IN WRITING FIRST: what you will deliver, how many sessions, how many participants, what materials, what the recording rights are, and what a cancellation costs.

That last point matters. If the client records your session and reuses it with future staff forever, you sold one session and they bought a permanent asset. Decide and state whether the recording may be reused, and price it if so.

PRICING, WITH A FLOOR

Build the price from your real costs, exactly as the tutoring lesson does: preparation hours, delivery hours, platform, data, materials, and the admin of invoicing and chasing payment. That is your floor.

Then price the OUTCOME for a corporate client - forty staff who can now do something they could not - rather than the hour. An organisation comparing your fee with a venue, catering and travel for forty people is not comparing it with an hourly rate.

THE HONEST SUMMARY

Webinars do not create an audience; they convert one. Every model above except corporate training depends on people who already trust you, which is the same truth stated in the affiliate, course-creation and illustration lessons in this catalogue.

So the realistic sequence for most people here: build a skill and a small reputation, deliver free sessions that are genuinely useful, let that produce referrals - and sell corporate training, which is the one route that pays properly without first requiring a large public following.

And your income is income. Keep the record from the first payment: date, client, gross, deductions, net, what for. Confirm your own position with KRA or an accountant rather than relying on any figure here, because the thresholds change.',
  practice_activity = 'Choose a model on arithmetic, then prepare the invoice before you need it.

Step 1. Write which of the four models you are pursuing, and one sentence of reasoning that refers to the audience you actually have today.

Step 2. Do the conversion arithmetic for the sell-at-the-end model with your own numbers:

- Registrations you can realistically get: ____
- Attendees at 40 percent: ____
- Buyers at 5 percent of attendees: ____
- Price of what you sell: KSh ____
- Revenue: KSh ____

Then double the price of what you sell and recalculate. Write one sentence on what that tells you about where to spend your effort.

Step 3. Subtract the real costs:

- Platform subscription this month, in shillings: ____
- Your data for rehearsal and event: ____
- Payment gateway percentage: ____
- Speaker fee: ____
- Preparation hours, and what an hour of your time is worth: ____
- TRUE PROFIT: KSh ____

Step 4. Name three real organisations near you with a training budget and a staff skill gap you could fill. By name. For each, write the one session you would deliver and who the decision-maker is.

Step 5. Write the corporate proposal on one page: what you deliver, how many sessions, how many participants, what materials, recording rights, cancellation terms, and the fee.

Step 6. Settle the recording question explicitly. Write whether the client may reuse the recording with future staff, and if so what that costs extra. This is the clause people forget and regret.

Step 7. Build your invoice template now: your name, KRA PIN, client details, description, date, amount, payment details. Write it out once so it exists before a client asks.

Step 8. Plan the cash-flow reality. Write the payment terms you expect, the date you would actually be paid for a session delivered this week, and what you will do in the meantime.

Step 9. Write one line each naming what you will confirm with KRA about VAT registration, withholding tax on professional fees, and declaring this income.

Step 10. Start the income record: date, client, gross, deductions, net, what for. Write where it lives.',
  knowledge_check = '[{"question": "Which webinar model is the most reliable income in Kenya, and why?", "options": ["Ticket sales, because the money arrives immediately", "Sponsorship, because companies have large budgets", "Corporate training, because organisations have allocated training budgets, one decision-maker, and repeat cohorts - with no audience-building funnel required", "Selling a low-priced product to a general audience at the end"], "correct_index": 2}, {"question": "You deliver a corporate training session this week. When should you expect to be paid?", "options": ["On the day, by M-Pesa", "Within a week of invoicing", "Typically 30 to 60 days after a correctly issued invoice, often following a purchase order, and possibly net of withholding tax", "Half before and half immediately after"], "correct_index": 2}]'::jsonb
where id = 'adbe9c21-7471-4e9f-b169-24f1b66dbd29';
